WEBVTT

NOTE
Canonical written narration timed from the exact local narration render receipt.
Pronunciation-only substitutions are intentionally absent from caption text.

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.634
This chapter asks a specific question: AI-assisted science closes a loop

2
00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:11.209
from research objective and hypothesis through design, simulation or instrument control,

3
00:00:11.209 --> 00:00:15.770
measurement, analysis, causal or statistical claim, replication, dual-use review,

4
00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:17.290
and evidence handoff.

5
00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:21.759
Generic planning and tool use do not preserve the epistemic controls

6
00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:23.260
of that full loop.

7
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:29.131
The tempting shortcut is insufficient: A fluent hypothesis, autonomous laboratory,

8
00:00:29.131 --> 00:00:35.215
successful synthesis, simulator result, significant p-value, or generated paper can be

9
00:00:35.215 --> 00:00:41.299
useful while still hiding HARKing, selective stopping, instrument drift, contaminated controls,

10
00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:42.405
analysis flexibility,

11
00:00:42.745 --> 00:00:47.575
failed experiments, human intervention, replication gaps, or dual-use risk.

12
00:00:48.175 --> 00:00:53.587
The chapter's core claim is this: An AI-generated scientific claim should

13
00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:59.000
enter the evidence stack only through a preregistered experimental contract that

14
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.920
binds hypothesis lineage, exploratory versus confirmatory status, design and power,

15
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.380
instrument or simulator authority, calibration,

16
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:13.153
sample and protocol lineage, blinding and holdouts, stopping and exclusions, analysis,

17
00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:19.586
complete attempts, independent replication, dual-use disposition, and claim ceiling; experimental completion,

18
00:01:19.586 --> 00:01:21.925
significance, synthesis, instrument output,

19
00:01:22.265 --> 00:01:27.471
or formal workflow validity alone establishes neither causal truth,

20
00:01:27.471 --> 00:01:31.520
general scientific discovery, reproducibility, safety, nor transfer.

21
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:38.738
Record hypothesis ancestry, prior evidence, competing explanations, exploratory status, preregistration, design,

22
00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:45.615
power, outcomes, exclusions, stopping, and analysis before protected data are opened.

23
00:01:46.215 --> 00:01:49.947
A concrete implementation trace makes the proposal testable.

24
00:01:49.947 --> 00:01:55.079
Use a benign reproducible simulation or low-risk open instrument with injected

25
00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:56.945
null and known effects.

26
00:01:57.285 --> 00:02:02.677
Freeze stopping and analysis, preserve every attempt, blind the confirmatory holdout,

27
00:02:02.677 --> 00:02:07.578
run independent analysis, and package protocol, data, code, calibration, exclusions,

28
00:02:07.578 --> 00:02:11.989
and residuals for replication. This tests the control plane,

29
00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:14.440
not autonomous science in general.

30
00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:20.408
Lease simulators and instruments with calibration, operating envelope, sample identity,

31
00:02:20.408 --> 00:02:24.910
control state, maintenance, operator intervention, and safety authority.

32
00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.978
Separate generated hypotheses, execution, measurement, analysis, causal interpretation,

33
00:02:29.978 --> 00:02:36.480
and claim drafting across inspectable roles and preserve all failed attempts.

34
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.133
The design can still fail. HARKing p-hacking selective stopping publication bias

35
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:46.267
The correct response is to stop, narrow, quarantine, compensate,

36
00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:50.860
or retain an owned residual rather than narrate uncertainty away.

37
00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:56.506
This chapter remains Design rationale at argument support. Its contracts, examples,

38
00:02:56.506 --> 00:03:00.635
tests, or local artifacts establish only their encoded scope.

39
00:03:00.635 --> 00:03:05.681
The chapter names proof targets rather than pretending they are already

40
00:03:05.681 --> 00:03:06.140
closed.

41
00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.795
A finite Scientific Discovery and Experimental Governance record may hand off

42
00:03:11.795 --> 00:03:17.111
only when identity, authority, version, required checks, and residual ownership are

43
00:03:17.111 --> 00:03:21.460
present; no theorem grants empirical effectiveness or release authority.

44
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.347
Simulator or instrument return is an observation, not scientific truth.

45
00:03:27.347 --> 00:03:31.230
Exploratory and confirmatory analyses remain visibly distinct.

46
00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:36.794
No chapter claim or evidence state is promoted by this visual.

47
00:03:36.794 --> 00:03:41.758
A named mechanism is not proof of correct implementation or useful

48
00:03:41.758 --> 00:03:46.722
deployment. A local check or formal model does not establish real-world

49
00:03:46.722 --> 00:03:51.235
enforcement. No safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI, or ASI conclusion follows.

50
00:03:51.575 --> 00:03:56.050
These boundaries matter because an explanatory derivative is useful only if

51
00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:59.305
it preserves the evidence ceiling of its source.

52
00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:05.053
At most, this visual explains the chapter's design rationale at argument

53
00:04:05.053 --> 00:04:09.733
support; it adds no empirical, deployment, safety, transfer, state-of-the-art, AGI,

54
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:14.881
or ASI result. The next chapter is Artifact Steward Agents and

55
00:04:14.881 --> 00:04:16.285
Living Project Governance.

56
00:04:16.625 --> 00:04:21.586
It takes responsibility for Durable artifacts need continuity across planning, funding,

57
00:04:21.586 --> 00:04:26.547
work assignment, release, maintenance, governance, and eventual sunset without turning an

58
00:04:26.547 --> 00:04:30.155
AI manager into the owner of the project.
